WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.395
Here is a box sliding at a steady speed. The push

2
00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:07.575
forward exactly balances friction backward.

3
00:00:08.025 --> 00:00:10.150
The resultant force is zero,

4
00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:16.525
so the velocity does not change. Motion does not need a force; changing motion does.

5
00:00:17.425 --> 00:00:21.647
Now push harder. Forty newtons forward against twenty newtons

6
00:00:21.647 --> 00:00:25.400
of friction leaves a resultant of twenty newtons.

7
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:31.600
The box accelerates: its velocity rises steadily, as the graph shows.

8
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:38.175
Double the mass, with the same resultant force, and the acceleration halves.

9
00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:42.075
A heavier object is harder to speed up.

10
00:00:42.975 --> 00:00:49.850
Newton’s second law puts it together: resultant force equals mass times acceleration.

11
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:55.209
A resultant force of thirty newtons on a six kilogram trolley

12
00:00:55.209 --> 00:00:59.225
gives an acceleration of five metres per second squared.
